ic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For integers $0\\le k\\le n$, which formula equals $\\binom{n}{k}$ for every such pair?","options":["$\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}$","$\\frac{n!}{k!}$","$n^k$","$\\frac{n!}{k!\\,(n-k)!}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"33c92f30d941d1f1b52ca23e","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_003_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For integers $n\\ge2$ and $1\\le k\\le n-1$, which identity holds for every such pair?","options":["$\\binom{n}{k}=\\binom{n-1}{k-1}\\binom{n-1}{k}$","$\\binom{n}{k}=\\binom{n-1}{k}+\\binom{n}{k-1}$","$\\binom{n}{k}=\\binom{n+1}{k+1}$","$\\binom{n}{k}=\\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6f75c6702c3a1ff568db91a1","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_010_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ be the number of successes in $n$ independent trials, so that $\\mathbb{P}(X=k)=\\binom{n}{k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k}$. What does the factor $\\binom{n}{k}$ represent?","options":["The number of success/failure sequences of length $n$ with exactly $k$ successes","The probability of one particular sequence with exactly $k$ successes","The number of orders in which the $k$ successes can be arranged among themselves","The number of all success/failure sequences of length $n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eb32762ab7ee4a61c75abcb8","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_012_curated20261027_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What are $\\binom{4}{0}$, $\\binom{4}{1}$ and $\\binom{4}{4}$, in this order?","options":["$1,\\ 4,\\ 1$","$0,\\ 4,\\ 1$","$1,\\ 4,\\ 24$","$0,\\ 4,\\ 0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3125f6588d598a715a5d5ff3","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_013_curated20261027_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":